The menu bar, Cmd-Tab and the Dock show the game's name, not "wine".

From CodeWeavers' CrossOver 26.3.0 sources, CW HACK 22144 in dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
(LGPL-2.1-or-later), adapted as described below: every Windows program starts through a symbolic link
named after it.

macOS's window server takes a program's name once, from the file name it was started through, and
never changes it. Wine's loader file is called "wine", so the menu bar and Cmd-Tab read "wine" for every
game, even after patch 0006 renames the process in LaunchServices. Renaming later (0006's call, the
private CPSSetProcessName, NSProcessInfo, argv[0]) does not reach the window server; the file name does.

CrossOver already has this route: CW HACK 22144 (CodeWeavers, in dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c) execs each
Windows program through a link named after it, in a per-loader folder in the user's temp folder. This
patch adapts that hack and keeps its structure, so CodeWeavers' later changes still apply:

- No WINEDLLPATH condition. The hack ran only with WINEDLLPATH set, because a hard link needs ntdll.so
  beside it. A symbolic link does not: the loader and ntdll find their folder through the link's real
  path.
- A symbolic link, not a hard link. The process's executable path (proc_pidpath) then stays the loader
  inside the engine, which a launcher uses to find and stop the processes it started, and to know an
  engine folder is in use. Through a hard link the process would sit in the temp folder. CrossOver
  chose the hard link to avoid the Dock's alias arrow; this patch accepts that risk.
- The name is WINEPRELOADERAPPNAME when it is set (a launcher's game title, which patch 0006 keeps for
  the program's children), and it is never overwritten. Only when it is unset is the name the
  program's file name, and then the variable is not set from it, so a launcher's own name does not
  pass to the game it starts.
- The name is made safe for a file name and for the window server: "/" becomes U+2215 (division
  slash), control characters (C0, DEL and C1) are dropped, and it is cut to 31 bytes between whole
  characters (the window server keeps 31 bytes, and none of a name cut inside a character). "." and
  ".." are not used.
- A name already in the folder that is not a link to this loader (a link to an engine that moved, a
  hard link, a file) is replaced in one step, through a temporary name and rename(). EEXIST from a
  program started at the same moment counts as success.
- If the link cannot be made or exec'd, the loader is exec'd directly, as before; patch 0006's
  LaunchServices rename then still names the Dock.
- extract_exe_name returned the file name with its extension, though its comment says it follows
  CW hack 13438's rule (which drops it); it now drops the extension, so the link and the app menu
  agree ("notepad", not "notepad.exe").

WINELOADERNOEXEC, which loader_exec sets before every exec, still keeps the started loader from
reading its file name as a builtin program: a game titled "Control" starts the game, not Wine's
control panel. The dockname gate checks that title.

Upstream status: the change to CW HACK 22144 is offered to CodeWeavers. WineHQ's loader has no such
hack.

